      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-159685",
      ":question/problem": "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$\u03c3_{1}^{2} = 1, \u03c3_{2}^{2} = 4, \u03c1 = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{k}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - \u03bc)^{T}\u03a3^{-1}(x - \u03bc))$,\n\nwhere $\u03bc$ is the mean vector and $\u03a3$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k$."
